<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  The sandbox, and the one grant it needs.

  One file for three bundles, because nothing in it is per-application: each
  opens the file a person chose and writes nothing at all.

  **The entitlement does nothing until it is inside a signature.** An unsigned
  bundle is not sandboxed, so every measurement of sandbox behaviour has to be
  made against a signed one, and an Apple Development certificate is enough for
  that. This is why `build-app.sh` signs even for a development build, and why
  nothing about the sandbox can be settled from Linux.

  `files.user-selected.read-write` rather than read-only, although these
  applications write nothing today. The grant is what an open panel hands over,
  and read-write is the grant the editing work will need for the save path. A
  capability asked for and unused is a question at review with no good answer, so
  this is the one place the rule is bent, and it is bent by one word rather than
  by a whole key: a reader that asked only to read would have to re-ask, and
  re-asking changes the sandbox container a person's grants live in.

  Nothing else. No network, no broad filesystem access, no device, and no
  keychain group even though the provisioning profile grants one.

  Author: David M. Anderson
  Built with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic)
-->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
